proofs=[{'id':'cross-condition','title':'Certifying a whole AC subspace','summary':'Every ordered pair of basis vectors must obey the cross reduced-state identity. Testing the individual vectors alone is insufficient.','formulaLatex':r'\operatorname{Tr}_{N-t}(|u_a\rangle\langle u_b|)=\delta_{ab}\frac{I_{\mathrm{Sym}^t}}{t+1}', 'bodyHtml':'<p>For an orthonormal basis, expanding an arbitrary normalized superposition proves the condition sufficient. Polarization of the diagonal reduced-state identity proves it necessary. Equivalently, '+plain_math(r'B^\dagger T_{LM}B=0')+' for every rank '+plain_math(r'1\le L\le t')+'.</p>','references':[ref(3,'10–12')]}]
for b in paper['bounds']:
 proofs.append({'id':b['id'],'title':'Complementary Schmidt-space bound' if b['id']=='paper-schmidt-bound' else 'Spin-tensor Gram dimension bound','summary':b['proof'],'formulaLatex':b['formulaLatex'].replace('\\\\','\\'),'bodyHtml':'<p>'+html.escape(b.get('attribution','Published in Eq. (52); the complementary Schmidt-space argument applies to every t-AC subspace.'))+'</p>','references':[ref(9,'52')] if b['id']=='paper-schmidt-bound' else [ref(3,'10–12')]})
proofs.extend([
 {'id':'n17-third-order-maximum','title':'Seventeen qubits: an analytical third-order AC plane','summary':'A two-parameter radical construction gives an exact two-dimensional third-order subspace and reconstructs the numerical Julia example within its precision.', 'formulaLatex':r'K(17,3)=2', 'bodyHtml':'<p>The two basis vectors occupy Dicke residues zero and one modulo four. Rational probabilities and a closed quadrilateral of complex phases give every ordered identity '+plain_math(r'R_3(|u_a\rangle\langle u_b|)=\delta_{ab}I_4/4')+'. The catalogue displays a simple exact member of the analytical family; a separate rational-parameter choice reconstructs the supplied numerical plane to its reported precision.</p><p>The general rectangular-tensor bound '+plain_math(r'6k\le17')+' excludes dimension three. Hence the analytical plane has optimal dimension.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'Radical basis, analytical family and proof','url':'research/n17-t3/index.html'},{'label':'Exact coefficient expressions','url':'research/n17-t3/basis.json'},{'label':'Independent physical-spin verification','url':'research/n17-t3/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n18-second-order-maximum','title':'Eighteen qubits: an analytical second-order four-dimensional subspace','summary':'Four explicit radical Dicke vectors obey all ordered cross reductions and attain the dimension-four upper bound.', 'formulaLatex':r'K(18,2)=4', 'bodyHtml':'<p>After a collective rotation and a change of basis within the reported numerical span, the construction separates into even and odd reflection vectors on Dicke residue zero modulo three and a reflected pair on residues one and two. Their exact radical coefficients satisfy '+plain_math(r'R_2(|u_a\rangle\langle u_b|)=\delta_{ab}I_3/3')+'.</p><p>The auxiliary-spin bound '+plain_math(r'4k\le N-2=16')+' proves that dimension four is maximal. The reconstruction is compared numerically with the supplied Julia plane; exact existence follows from the symbolic identities.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'Explicit radical basis and optimality proof','url':'research/n18-t2/index.html'},{'label':'Exact coefficient expressions','url':'research/n18-t2/basis.json'},{'label':'Independent full-span verification','url':'research/n18-t2/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n19-second-order-maximum','title':'Nineteen qubits: an exact algebraic second-order four-dimensional subspace','summary':'An isolated root of eighteen rational quadratic equations defines four analytical basis vectors. A rational contraction certificate proves existence and every cross condition.', 'formulaLatex':r'K(19,2)=4', 'bodyHtml':'<p>Two sparse vectors and their coefficient reversals occupy the four Dicke residues modulo four. Eighteen real algebraic parameters are uniquely specified by rational quadratic equations and an explicit rational isolation box. An exact contraction certificate proves a zero in that box. An independent verifier reconstructs all 144 ordered two-qubit cross entries and proves that they vanish at the certified root.</p><p>This is an exact algebraic definition; a short expression using radicals is not claimed. The upper bound '+plain_math(r'4k\le N-2=17')+' excludes dimension five, proving the certified dimension four optimal.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'Algebraic basis, root definition and proof','url':'research/n19-t2/index.html'},{'label':'Exact coefficient expressions and parameter intervals','url':'research/n19-t2/basis.json'},{'label':'Independent rational existence and cross-reduction verification','url':'research/n19-t2/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n16-third-order-maximum','title':'Sixteen qubits: an analytical third-order AC plane','summary':'Two explicit radical Dicke vectors satisfy every ordered cross-reduction identity. They attain the dimension-two upper bound.', 'formulaLatex':r'K(16,3)=2', 'bodyHtml':'<p>The basis uses Dicke indices '+plain_math(r'0,4,8,12,16')+' and '+plain_math(r'2,6,10,14')+'. Exact radical phases cancel every off-diagonal cross condition, giving '+plain_math(r'R_3(|u_a\rangle\langle u_b|)=\delta_{ab}I_4/4')+'. Therefore every normalized superposition in the two-dimensional span is third-order AC.</p><p>The rectangular-tensor upper bound '+plain_math(r'6k\le16')+' excludes dimension three. The analytical construction proves the matching lower bound. It reconstructs the supplied numerical Julia example up to a common rotation and individual basis phases within the stated numerical accuracy.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'Explicit radical basis and complete proof','url':'research/n16-t3/index.html'},{'label':'LaTeX proof','url':'research/n16-t3/proof.tex'},{'label':'Independent full-span verification','url':'research/n16-t3/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n17-fourth-order-maximum','title':'Seventeen qubits: analytical fourth-order states and a saturation obstruction','summary':'Six explicit radical Dicke coefficients give fourth-order AC states. A hypothetical dimension-two code would lift to a pure 32-qubit state of AC order 15, contradicting tensor-Gram counting.', 'formulaLatex':r'K(17,4)=1', 'bodyHtml':'<p>The analytical state and its conjugate have '+plain_math(r'\rho_4=I_5/5')+' and proper rotational symmetry '+plain_math(r'D_3')+'. They are distinct one-dimensional representatives, and differ from the generic numerical Julia example.</p><p>Dimension two would saturate '+plain_math(r'9k=N+1=18')+'. The exact rank-two tensor channel then forces the code projector to have only scalar and rank-16 multipoles. The Schmidt-partner operator '+plain_math(r'Q=2\operatorname{Tr}_1P')+' is a rank-four projector on the 17-dimensional symmetric space of sixteen qubits, again with only scalar and rank-16 multipoles.</p><p>Writing '+plain_math(r'Q=4I_{17}/17+Y_{16}')+', time-reversed vectorization of '+plain_math(r'Y_{16}')+' produces a pure symmetric 32-qubit state with maximally mixed 15-qubit reduction. Its 64 orthogonal spin-tensor images cannot fit in dimension 33. This excludes every complex dimension-two fourth-order AC subspace, without a symmetry assumption.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'Analytical states and complete exclusion proof','url':'research/n17-t4/index.html'},{'label':'LaTeX proof','url':'research/n17-t4/proof.tex'},{'label':'Independent exact state and bound verification','url':'research/n17-t4/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n14-fourth-order-maximum','title':'Fourteen qubits: analytical fourth-order AC states','summary':'Five explicit radical Dicke coefficients reconstruct the numerical Julia example. This state and its rotation-inequivalent conjugate have four-qubit reduction I/5, proving that the maximum fourth-order AC-subspace dimension is one.', 'formulaLatex':r'K(14,4)=1', 'bodyHtml':'<p>In a rotated convention the only nonzero coefficients are '+plain_math(r'c_1=c_{13}=A,\ c_4=c_{10}=B,\ c_7=C')+'. The analytical expressions and exact checks are given in the linked derivation. Complex conjugation supplies a second, rotation-inequivalent branch. Both have proper rotational symmetry group '+plain_math(r'D_3')+'.</p><p>Direct normalized-Dicke splitting gives '+plain_math(r'\rho_4=I_5/5')+'. The spin-tensor Gram bound gives '+plain_math(r'9k\le15')+', so each one-dimensional span is optimal. The fifth reduction has purity '+plain_math(r'\operatorname{Tr}(\rho_5^2)=15209/90750>1/6')+': these states have AC order exactly four. Existence of a different fifth-order state at fourteen qubits remains open in the catalogue.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'Radical expressions and complete derivation','url':'research/n14-t4/index.html'},{'label':'Exact coefficients','url':'research/n14-t4/basis.json'},{'label':'LaTeX proof','url':'research/n14-t4/proof.tex'},{'label':'Independent exact verification','url':'research/n14-t4/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n20-second-order-maximum','title':'Twenty qubits: the second-order maximum is four','summary':'A rational contraction certificate proves existence of a four-dimensional second-order AC subspace. The earlier auxiliary spin-3/2 upper bound excludes dimension five.', 'formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(20,2)=4', 'bodyHtml':'<p>The four basis vectors have fixed even or odd Dicke support. With coefficients '+plain_math(r'c_{a,r}=\sqrt{\binom{20}{r}}(x_{a,r}+iy_{a,r})')+', the full normalization and first- and second-order compression conditions become 72 real quadratic equations with integer coefficients.</p><p>Twelve coordinates are fixed rationally; the other 72 are defined as the unique root in an explicit rational box. Exact integer bounds prove that the preconditioned Newton map is a strict contraction and maps the box into itself. The independent verifier reconstructs every physical moment equation and checks that the resulting span satisfies all ordered two-qubit cross reductions.</p><p>This is an exact algebraic existence certificate, with decimal coefficients supplied for visualization. A short radical formula is not asserted. The bound '+plain_math(r'4k\le20-2')+' gives '+plain_math(r'k\le4')+', so the certified construction is optimal.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'Basis, rational root certificate and complete proof','url':'research/n20-t2/index.html'},{'label':'Explicit algebraic Dicke coefficients','url':'research/n20-t2/basis.json'},{'label':'LaTeX proof','url':'research/n20-t2/proof.tex'},{'label':'Independent existence verification','url':'research/n20-t2/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'second-order-spin2-inertia','title':'A stronger second-order bound from auxiliary spin two','summary':'A quadratic spin coupling gives 5k≤N+5 for every N≥4 and AC order at least two. Together with 4k≤N−2 for N≥8, it improves the asymptotic upper bound from N/4 to N/5.', 'formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(N,t)\le\min\!\left\{\left\lfloor\frac{N-2}{4}\right\rfloor,\left\lfloor\frac{N+5}{5}\right\rfloor\right\}\quad(N\ge8,\ t\ge2)', 'bodyHtml':'<p>Let '+plain_math(r'j=N/2')+' and couple to auxiliary spin '+plain_math(r'2')+'. The second-order AC identities give zero compression of '+plain_math(r'W=X^2+(j+3)X-2j(j+1)I')+', where '+plain_math(r'X=\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf S')+', on '+plain_math(r'\mathcal C\otimes\mathbb C^5')+'.</p><p>For '+plain_math(r'j\ge2')+', the five eigenvalues, in increasing total-spin order, are '+plain_math(r'-2(j+1),\ -2j(j+1),\ -j(2j+5),\ -5j-2,\ 4j(j+1)')+'. Only the highest-spin sector is positive, and it has dimension '+plain_math(r'N+5')+'. Injective projection of the code-ancilla space into that sector therefore proves '+plain_math(r'5k\le N+5')+'.</p><p>The earlier fourth-denominator bound cannot be sharp for all sizes: at '+plain_math(r'N=34')+' it permits dimension eight, whereas this bound gives seven. When this bound was introduced, '+plain_math(r'(N,t)=(42,2)')+' improves from '+plain_math(r'4\le K\le10')+' to '+plain_math(r'4\le K\le9')+'. General attainment of the combined bound is not proved.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'General second-order proof and sharpness status','url':'research/t2-spin2-inertia/index.html'},{'label':'LaTeX proof','url':'research/t2-spin2-inertia/proof.tex'},{'label':'Independent exact verification','url':'research/t2-spin2-inertia/independent-verification.json'},{'label':'Earlier auxiliary spin-3/2 bound','url':'research/ancilla-inertia/index.html'}]},
 {'id':'first-order-optimality','title':'First-order maximum for every qubit number','summary':'An auxiliary spin-1 inertia bound gives 3k≤N+3. The analytical reflection and central blocks attain floor(N/3)+1 for every N≥4, proving the entire first-order sequence.', 'formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(N,1)=\left\lfloor\frac N3\right\rfloor+1\qquad(N\ge4)', 'bodyHtml':'<p>For a first-order AC projector '+plain_math(r'P')+', polarization gives '+plain_math(r'PJ_aP=0')+' for all three spin components. Couple spin '+plain_math(r'j=N/2')+' to an auxiliary spin '+plain_math(r'1')+' and set '+plain_math(r'W=\mathbf J\cdot\mathbf S')+'. Its compression to '+plain_math(r'\mathcal C\otimes\mathbb C^3')+' is zero.</p><p>The eigenvalues of '+plain_math(r'W')+' are '+plain_math(r'j,-1,-(j+1)')+', with multiplicities '+plain_math(r'N+3,N+1,N-1')+'. There is no zero eigenspace. Projection of the '+plain_math(r'3k')+'-dimensional code-ancilla space into the positive eigenspace is injective: a nonzero vector in the kernel would have strictly negative expectation. Thus '+plain_math(r'3k\le N+3')+'.</p><p>The existing analytical construction supplies the matching lower bound for every '+plain_math(r'N\ge4')+'. The small cases are '+plain_math(r'k_{\max}(1,1)=0')+' and '+plain_math(r'k_{\max}(2,1)=k_{\max}(3,1)=1')+'. This proof covers arbitrary complex subspaces.</p>', 'references':[{'label':'General proof, spectrum and all eleven settled entries','url':'research/t1-optimality/index.html'},{'label':'Standalone LaTeX proof','url':'research/t1-optimality/proof.tex'},{'label':'Matching analytical bases for every size','url':'research/analytical-constructions/index.html'},{'label':'Independent exact verification','url':'research/t1-optimality/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id': 'ancilla-inertia-bound', 'title': 'Auxiliary-spin inertia bound for second-order AC subspaces', 'summary': 'Coupling to spin 3/2 gives 4k≤N−2 for every N≥8. The bound also applies to every higher AC order.', 'formulaLatex': 'k_{\\max}(N,t)\\le\\left\\lfloor\\frac{N-2}{4}\\right\\rfloor\\qquad(N\\ge8,\\ t\\ge2)', 'bodyHtml': '<p>With <span data-tex="X=\\mathbf J\\cdot\\mathbf S"></span> and auxiliary spin <span data-tex="3/2"></span>, the operator <span data-tex="W_N=X^2-\\frac{N-4}{2}X-\\frac{5N(N+2)}{16}I"></span> has zero compression on <span data-tex="\\mathcal C\\otimes\\mathbb C^4"></span> for every second-order AC subspace. Its only positive eigenspace is total spin <span data-tex="(N-3)/2"></span>, of dimension <span data-tex="N-2"></span>; all other eigenvalues are strictly negative when <span data-tex="N\\ge8"></span>. Injectivity of projection into this positive eigenspace proves the bound.</p><p>This establishes the maxima <span data-tex="k_{\\max}(13,2)=2"></span> and <span data-tex="k_{\\max}(16,2)=k_{\\max}(17,2)=3"></span> using the existing exact witnesses. No numerical infeasibility or unproved equality classification is used.</p>', 'references': [{'label': 'General proof, spectrum and every improved entry', 'url': 'research/ancilla-inertia/index.html'}, {'label': 'General LaTeX proof', 'url': 'research/ancilla-inertia/proof.tex'}, {'label': 'Independent exact verification', 'url': 'research/ancilla-inertia/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n13-sparse-state-exclusion','title':'Thirteen qubits: any dimension-three second-order code must avoid all two-component Dicke states','summary':'An exact conditional certificate excludes every three-dimensional second-order AC subspace containing a state with at most two Dicke components along any axis. It is retained as an earlier conditional argument; the separate auxiliary-spin proof now settles the unrestricted maximum.','formulaLatex':r'\dim\mathcal C=3,\ t=2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad U^{\otimes13}u\notin\mathcal C\quad\forall U\in SU(2),\qquad u=\frac{\sqrt{10}D_0+\sqrt{26}D_9}{6}','bodyHtml':'<p>If the code contains '+plain_math(r'u')+', its other two basis vectors must lie in a seven-dimensional orthogonal kernel of the rank-at-most-two tensor images of '+plain_math(r'u')+'. Their exterior product lies in a 21-dimensional space. Averaging over the ninefold axial rotation gives 48 real invariant coordinates. Forty independent exact contraction equations leave an eight-dimensional kernel, annihilated by a strictly positive integer identity on all 21 block diagonals. Positivity gives a contradiction.</p><p>For a two-component second-order AC state, zero mean and the required variance imply '+plain_math(r'ab=65/4')+' for its positive and negative magnetic labels. The only allowed half-integer pair is '+plain_math(r'\{a,b\}=\{13/2,5/2\}')+'. Therefore every such state is a collective rotation of '+plain_math(r'u')+'. The proof covers arbitrary complex codes containing such a state; it does not prove that every code must contain one.</p>','references':[{'label':'Conditional exclusion and its precise scope','url':'research/n13-t2/index.html#sparse-state-exclusion'},{'label':'Complete conditional proof in LaTeX','url':'research/n13-t2/containing-u/proof.tex'},{'label':'Exact positive certificate','url':'research/n13-t2/containing-u/certificate.json'},{'label':'Independent physical verification','url':'research/n13-t2/containing-u/verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n13-first-order-wedge','title':'Thirteen qubits: first-order maximum dimension is five','summary':'An exact exterior-power certificate excludes dimension six. The analytical central-block construction attains dimension five.','formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(13,1)=5','bodyHtml':'<p>A hypothetical six-dimensional first-order AC subspace gives a normalized exterior vector in '+plain_math(r'\Lambda^6 V_{13/2}')+'. Its rotational average is positive and obeys the full first-leg spin-contraction equations.</p><p>The complete 3003-dimensional exterior representation has 735 real invariant block coordinates. There are 716 independent exact contraction equations, leaving a nineteen-dimensional kernel. A strictly positive integer combination of all 151 block diagonals vanishes on this kernel. Positivity therefore forces the entire operator to vanish, contradicting trace one.</p><p>Two exact verifiers reconstruct the physical equations and check the certificate. The proof applies to arbitrary complex subspaces. The existing five-dimensional analytical basis attains the upper bound.</p>','references':[{'label':'Complete proof and exact five-dimensional basis','url':'research/n13-t1/index.html'},{'label':'LaTeX proof','url':'research/n13-t1/proof.tex'},{'label':'Positive integer certificate','url':'research/n13-t1/certificate.json'},{'label':'Independent physical-word verification','url':'research/n13-t1/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n13-phase-family','title':'Thirteen qubits: an analytical family of second-order subspaces','summary':'A continuous family of inequivalent two-dimensional AC subspaces is verified symbolically. Every member attains the proved maximum dimension two.','formulaLatex':r'\mathcal C_\phi=\operatorname{span}\!\left\{\frac{\sqrt{10}D_0+\sqrt{26}D_9}{6},\frac{\sqrt{26}D_4+e^{i\phi}\sqrt{10}D_{13}}{6}\right\}','bodyHtml':'<p>Here '+plain_math(r'D_r=|D_{13}^{(r)}\rangle')+' and '+plain_math(r'\phi\in\mathbb R')+'. All 36 ordered two-qubit cross-reduction entries are verified for arbitrary phase. The rank-at-most-two tensor images span the full fourteen-dimensional space, so no third vector can be added to any member.</p><p>The invariant '+plain_math(r'\operatorname{Tr}(P_\phi\Theta P_\phi\Theta^{-1})=2-\frac{65}{81}(1+\cos\phi)')+' proves collective-rotation inequivalence for distinct '+plain_math(r'\phi\in[0,\pi]')+'. The separate auxiliary-spin inertia proof gives the matching global upper bound, so every member is optimal.</p>','references':[{'label':'Family, nonextension proof and remaining gap','url':'research/n13-t2/index.html#phase-family'},{'label':'Symbolic phase-family verification','url':'research/n13-t2/family-verification.json'},{'label':'Reproduce the symbolic check','url':'research/n13-t2/verify_family.py'}]},
 {'id':'rectangular-tensor-bound','title':'General dimension bound for every qubit number and AC order','summary':'Orthogonal images under rectangular spin tensors give a bound for every N and t, containing the Schmidt and tensor-Gram bounds and strengthening odd orders.','formulaLatex':r'k\,m_h(m_h+h)\le N-h+1,\quad m_h=\left\lfloor\frac{t-h}{2}\right\rfloor+1,\quad0\le h\le t','bodyHtml':'<p>Choose Hilbert–Schmidt orthonormal tensor maps from '+plain_math(r'\mathrm{Sym}^{N}(\mathbb C^2)')+' to '+plain_math(r'\mathrm{Sym}^{N-h}(\mathbb C^2)')+' with ranks '+plain_math(r'h/2,h/2+1,\ldots\le t/2')+'. Every pairwise product has tensor rank at most '+plain_math(r't')+', so the whole-subspace AC condition makes all images of all basis vectors orthogonal. Counting these nonzero vectors gives the displayed inequality.</p><p>Optimizing over '+plain_math(r'h')+' yields '+plain_math(r'k(r+1)(r+2)\le N')+' at odd order '+plain_math(r't=2r+1')+'. At even order '+plain_math(r't=2r\ge2')+', it yields '+plain_math(r'k(r+1)^2\le N+1')+' together with the necessary existence condition '+plain_math(r'N\ge(r+1)^2')+'. In particular, third order requires '+plain_math(r'6k\le N')+' and fifth order '+plain_math(r'12k\le N')+'.</p><p>The proof applies to arbitrary complex subspaces and every admissible size. Finite verification checks supplement the general argument.</p>','references':[{'label':'General formulas, full proof and all improved entries','url':'research/general-bounds/index.html'},{'label':'Complete LaTeX proof','url':'research/general-bounds/proof.tex'},{'label':'Independent rectangular-tensor verification','url':'research/general-bounds/rectangular-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'tensor-packing-saturation','title':'General equality obstructions for tensor dimension bounds','summary':'At equality the tensor images fill the output space. Exact covariant-channel multipliers then restrict the projector to specific spin ranks and can exclude saturation.','formulaLatex':r'kD=\dim\mathcal H_{\rm out}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad P_L=0\ \text{whenever}\ L>t\ \text{and}\ \lambda_L\ne0','bodyHtml':'<p>The channel multipliers are finite exact sums of Wigner '+plain_math(r'6j')+' symbols. For even order, equality is impossible if no nonscalar rank above '+plain_math(r't')+' has zero multiplier. At odd order, the only additional invisible rank is '+plain_math(r'N')+'. A projector with only scalar and rank-'+plain_math(r'N')+' components would lift to a pure symmetric '+plain_math(r'2N')+'-qubit state of order '+plain_math(r'N-1')+', excluded by the general dimension bound for every '+plain_math(r'N\ge7')+'. Thus a zero multiplier at an allowed lower rank is also necessary.</p><p>At third order, saturation '+plain_math(r'6k=N\ge12')+' requires an integer '+plain_math(r'4\le L\le N-1')+' with '+plain_math(r'2L(L+1)=(N-1)(N+2)')+'. At a pure odd-order equality, the next multipole must also have zero channel multiplier; otherwise the state has higher AC order and violates the even-order bound. This excludes '+plain_math(r'N=20,t=7')+'. A permitted root is only a necessary condition, not an existence proof.</p>','references':[{'label':'General equality proof and arithmetic tests','url':'research/general-bounds/index.html#equality'},{'label':'Exact channel verification and positive controls','url':'research/general-bounds/odd-saturation-verification.json'},{'label':'Independent third-order spectral proof','url':'research/general-bounds/t3-saturation-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n11-first-order-wedge','title':'Eleven qubits: first-order maximum dimension is four','summary':'An exact exterior-power certificate excludes every five-dimensional first-order AC subspace. The existing four-dimensional Julia basis attains the maximum.','formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(11,1)=4','bodyHtml':'<p>A hypothetical orthonormal basis '+plain_math(r'u_1,\ldots,u_5')+' gives '+plain_math(r'w=u_1\wedge\cdots\wedge u_5\in\Lambda^5V_{11/2}')+'. The full compressed-spin conditions imply '+plain_math(r'\operatorname{Tr}_1[(J_z\otimes I^{\otimes4})X]=0')+' for the positive, trace-one rotational average '+plain_math(r'X')+' of '+plain_math(r'|w\rangle\langle w|')+'.</p><p>The complete 792-dimensional exterior representation has 111 real invariant block coordinates. One hundred independent exact contraction equations leave an eleven-dimensional kernel. A strictly positive integer combination of all 49 block diagonals vanishes on this kernel. Positivity then forces every block to vanish, contradicting trace one.</p><p>Two separate exact verifiers reconstruct the physical equations and the integer certificate. The exclusion applies to arbitrary complex subspaces. The existing Julia basis has all 64 ordered one-qubit cross-reduction entries checked exactly, proving dimension four is attained.</p>','references':[{'label':'Complete proof and exact four-dimensional basis','url':'research/n11-t1/index.html'},{'label':'LaTeX proof','url':'research/n11-t1/proof.tex'},{'label':'Positive integer certificate','url':'research/n11-t1/certificate.json'},{'label':'Portable exact verification','url':'research/n11-t1/verification.json'},{'label':'Independent physical-word verification','url':'research/n11-t1/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id': 'n13-second-order-inertia', 'title': 'Thirteen qubits: second-order maximum dimension is two', 'summary': 'A spin-3/2 auxiliary system gives an elementary spectral proof excluding all three-dimensional second-order AC subspaces. The exact two-dimensional family attains the bound.', 'formulaLatex': '4k\\le11\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k_{\\max}(13,2)=2', 'bodyHtml': '<p>Put <span data-tex="X=\\sum_a J_a\\otimes S_a"></span>, where <span data-tex="J_a"></span> acts in spin <span data-tex="13/2"></span> and <span data-tex="S_a"></span> in spin <span data-tex="3/2"></span>. The whole-subspace second-order conditions imply zero compression of <span data-tex="W=X^2-\\frac92X-\\frac{975}{16}I"></span> to <span data-tex="\\mathcal C\\otimes\\mathbb C^4"></span>.</p><p>The eigenvalues on total spins <span data-tex="5,6,7,8"></span> are <span data-tex="465/4,-39/4,-263/4,-39/4"></span>, with multiplicities <span data-tex="11,13,15,17"></span>. Thus the Hermitian form has eleven positive and forty-five negative directions, and no zero directions. Projection of any subspace with zero compression into the positive eigenspace is injective; hence <span data-tex="4\\dim\\mathcal C\\le11"></span>. This excludes dimension three for arbitrary complex subspaces, without a symmetry or support assumption.</p><p>The exact two-dimensional sparse family supplies the matching lower bound.</p>', 'references': [{'label': 'Complete proof and optimal analytical family', 'url': 'research/n13-t2/index.html'}, {'label': 'Standalone LaTeX proof', 'url': 'research/n13-t2/inertia/proof.tex'}, {'label': 'Exact physical-spin verification', 'url': 'research/n13-t2/inertia/verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n9-second-order-wedge','title':'Nine qubits: second-order maximum dimension is one','summary':'An exact four-copy certificate excludes every two-dimensional second-order AC subspace. The antisymmetric vector of a basis preserves all cross conditions; positivity then contradicts its normalized moment.','formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(9,2)=1','bodyHtml':'<p>For a putative orthonormal pair '+plain_math(r'u,v')+', put '+plain_math(r'w=(u\otimes v-v\otimes u)/\sqrt2')+'. The whole-subspace conditions imply '+plain_math(r'\operatorname{Tr}_1[(T_{L0}\otimes I)|w\rangle\langle w|]=0')+' for tensor ranks '+plain_math(r'L=1,2')+'. Twirling '+plain_math(r'|w\otimes w\rangle\langle w\otimes w|')+' gives a positive, trace-one operator supported on the 825-dimensional Young-symmetry space '+plain_math(r'S_{(2,2)}(V_{9/2})')+'.</p><p>In a complete exact angular-momentum basis, 156 independent rational equations constrain 169 real invariant coordinates. A strictly positive integer combination of all 57 diagonal entries of the multiplicity blocks is identically zero. Positivity therefore makes every block vanish, contradicting trace one. The certificate and a separate physical-word reconstruction use exact arithmetic.</p><p>An existing exact second-order pure state supplies dimension one. No reality, Dicke-support or constellation-symmetry assumption is imposed on the excluded subspace.</p>','references':[{'label':'Complete four-copy proof and exact witness','url':'research/n9-t2/index.html'},{'label':'Positive integer certificate','url':'research/n9-t2/certificate.json'},{'label':'Portable exact verification','url':'research/n9-t2/verification.json'},{'label':'Independent physical-word audit','url':'research/n9-t2/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'first-order-saturation-obstruction','title':'First-order saturation is impossible at every even N≥8','summary':'A subspace saturating k=N/2 would lift to a pure symmetric 2N-qubit state of AC order N−1. An exact recoupling identity excludes this at N=8; tensor-Gram dimension counting excludes all larger even sizes.','formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(N,1)\le\frac N2-1\quad(N\ge8\text{ even})','bodyHtml':'<p>Equality in the spin-Casimir estimate forces '+plain_math(r'P=\frac{N}{2(N+1)}I+X')+', where '+plain_math(r'X')+' has only tensor rank '+plain_math(r'N')+'. Time-reversed vectorization of '+plain_math(r'X')+' produces a pure symmetric '+plain_math(r'2N')+'-qubit state with '+plain_math(r'(N-1)')+'-qubit reduction '+plain_math(r'I/N')+'.</p><p>For '+plain_math(r'N=8')+', exact two-copy recoupling of this hypothetical seventh-order AC state gives '+plain_math(r'0=100+136p_0+115a_9')+'. Here '+plain_math(r'p_0')+' is a total-spin probability and '+plain_math(r'a_9')+' a squared multipole norm, both nonnegative. For even '+plain_math(r'N\ge10')+', the lifted state would require '+plain_math(r'(N/2)^2\le2N+1')+', which is impossible.</p><p>Existing exact bases make the new bound sharp at '+plain_math(r'N=8,10,12')+', with dimensions '+plain_math(r'3,4,5')+' respectively.</p>','references':[{'label':'Complete vectorization proof and eight-qubit classification','url':'research/n8/index.html#first-order'},{'label':'Exact positive identity and conventions','url':'research/n8/t1/certificate.json'},{'label':'Exact vectorization and recoupling verification','url':'research/n8/t1/verification.json'},{'label':'Independent direct tensor audit','url':'research/n8/t1/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n8-second-order-complement','title':'Eight qubits: second- and third-order maximum dimension is one','summary':'A hypothetical dimension-two second-order subspace leaves a rank-one complement to its four spin-error images. Its exact multipole identities force a negative squared norm.','formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(8,2)=k_{\max}(8,3)=1','bodyHtml':'<p>For a putative rank-two projector '+plain_math(r'P')+', the operator '+plain_math(r'Q=I-P-\frac3{20}\sum_aJ_aPJ_a')+' is a rank-one projector orthogonal to '+plain_math(r'P')+'. It is second-order AC. Recovering '+plain_math(r'P')+' from the multipoles of '+plain_math(r'Q')+' and imposing '+plain_math(r'\operatorname{Tr}(PQ)=0')+' and '+plain_math(r'\operatorname{Tr}(P^2)=2')+' gives</p><div data-tex="0=5831+217971a_5" data-display="true"></div><p>The squared rank-five multipole norm '+plain_math(r'a_5\ge0')+' makes this impossible. The existing exact third-order AC state attains dimension one at both orders. The exclusion applies to arbitrary complex subspaces.</p>','references':[{'label':'Complete complement argument and exact lower witness','url':'research/n8/index.html#second-order'},{'label':'Rational certificate','url':'research/n8/t2/certificate.json'},{'label':'Exact independent recoupling verification','url':'research/n8/t2/verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'three-dimensional-second-order','title':'Exact three-dimensional second-order subspaces at N=14, 15 and 17','summary':'A radical tetrahedral construction at fourteen qubits and uniquely isolated algebraic solutions at fifteen and seventeen qubits supply exact dimension-three witnesses. The dimension-three upper bounds at N=14, N=15 and N=17 are attained.','formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(14,2)=k_{\max}(15,2)=k_{\max}(17,2)=3', 'bodyHtml':'<p>The fourteen-qubit coefficients are nested square roots. The fifteen- and seventeen-qubit coefficients are defined by rational polynomial systems and exact rational isolating boxes. A contraction proof establishes a unique real algebraic root in each box, and every cross reduced-state identity is an exact consequence of its defining equations.</p><p>These are exact algebraic existence certificates. The decimal components are previews of the certified roots; simple radical formulas are not asserted for the two implicit constructions.</p>','references':[{'label':'Bases, equations and complete existence proofs','url':'research/algebraic-k3/index.html'},{'label':'Fourteen-qubit verification','url':'research/algebraic-k3/n14/verification.json'},{'label':'Fifteen-qubit verification','url':'research/algebraic-k3/n15/verification.json'},{'label':'Independent rational root-certificate audit','url':'research/algebraic-k3/independent-certificate-audit.json'},{'label':'Seventeen-qubit verification','url':'research/algebraic-k3/n17/verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'first-order-analytic-family','title':'Optimal analytical first-order subspaces for every N≥4','summary':'Reflection pairs and central blocks give explicit exact bases of dimension floor(N/3)+1 for every N≥4. This attains all positive first-order values plotted in the paper, with the established N=2 and N=3 examples handling the small sizes.','formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(N,1)\ge\left\lfloor\frac N3\right\rfloor+1\quad(N\ge4)', 'bodyHtml':'<p>Each reflection pair has zero compressed spin operators. Separate the pairs by unused magnetic levels to eliminate cross terms. Three- and four-dimensional central blocks fill the remaining odd-size cases, with unit phases fixed by explicit radical formulas.</p><p>The auxiliary spin-1 upper bound now proves these analytical subspaces optimal for every N≥4. See the general first-order optimality proof.</p>','references':[{'label':'General construction, exact bases and proof','url':'research/analytical-constructions/index.html'},{'label':'Independent verification of all first-order bases','url':'research/analytical-constructions/t1-verification.json'},ref(8,'Figure 2')]},
 {'id':'gram-saturation-obstruction','title':'When the spin-error dimension bound cannot be saturated','summary':'If four times the code dimension fills the Hilbert space, the Casimir map forces a specific tensor rank to survive. If no such integer rank exists, equality is impossible.','formulaLatex':r'4k=N+1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 3L(L+1)=2N(N+2)\text{ for some }1\le L\le N', 'bodyHtml':'<p>For a second-order AC projector '+plain_math(r'P')+', equality in the orthogonal spin-error bound gives '+plain_math(r'I=P+\Phi(P)/c')+', where '+plain_math(r'\Phi(P)=\sum_aJ_aPJ_a')+' and '+plain_math(r'c=j(j+1)/3')+'. Its rank-'+plain_math(r'L')+' tensor component obeys '+plain_math(r'[4-3L(L+1)/(2j(j+1))]P_L=0')+'. If no allowed rank cancels this coefficient, '+plain_math(r'P=I/4')+', contradicting idempotence.</p><p>At eleven qubits this excludes dimension three. The new exact dimension-two basis therefore proves '+plain_math(r'k_{\max}(11,2)=2')+'.</p>','references':[{'label':'Complete argument and eleven-qubit basis','url':'research/analytical-constructions/index.html'},{'label':'Exact saturation checks','url':'research/analytical-constructions/gram-saturation-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'radical-reconstructions','title':'Exact radical reconstructions from the Julia supports','summary':'Exact two-dimensional subspaces at (N,t)=(12,2),(13,3),(18,3), obtained from rational diagonal moments and algebraic phase constraints.','formulaLatex':r'(N,k,t)=(12,2,2),\ (13,2,3),\ (18,2,3)', 'bodyHtml':'<p>The support gaps remove most off-diagonal terms. Rational moment equations fix the probabilities; the remaining cross equations reduce to unit-circle phase equations. Every resulting matrix entry is checked exactly. These are exact constructions with the source support patterns, not claims of exact equality to the original numerical subspaces.</p><p>For thirteen qubits, '+plain_math(r'k\le\lfloor(13-3+1)/4\rfloor=2')+' proves maximality. The twelve-qubit second-order construction is also maximal, by the rank-one complement exclusion below. At eighteen qubits the general third-order equality obstruction excludes dimension three; the exact dimension-two basis therefore proves maximality.</p>','references':[{'label':'Radical coefficients and derivations','url':'research/acliege/radical-subspaces/bases.json'},{'label':'Independent verification','url':'research/paper/radical-independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n12-t2-complement','title':'Twelve qubits: maximal second-order dimension is two','summary':'A hypothetical three-dimensional subspace would have a pure one-dimensional complement to its four mutually orthogonal spin-error spaces. An exact recoupling identity makes that complement impossible.','formulaLatex':r'k_{\max}(12,2)=2', 'bodyHtml':'<p>Let '+plain_math(r'P')+' project onto a putative three-dimensional second-order AC subspace. The twelve orthonormal vectors obtained from its basis by '+plain_math(r'I,J_x/\sqrt{14},J_y/\sqrt{14},J_z/\sqrt{14}')+' leave a rank-one projector '+plain_math(r'Q=I-P-\frac1{14}\sum_aJ_aPJ_a')+'. It is itself second-order AC and obeys '+plain_math(r'PQ=0')+'.</p><p>Exact spin-tensor recoupling then forces</p><div data-tex="0=9015+1031316a_3+3936270p_0+20020p_2+728365p_6" data-display="true"></div><p>Here '+plain_math(r'a_3')+' is a squared multipole norm of the complementary pure state, and '+plain_math(r'p_J')+' are total-spin probabilities of two copies. Every term is nonnegative and the constant is positive: a contradiction. The argument covers arbitrary complex subspaces. The displayed exact dimension-two basis therefore attains the maximum.</p>','references':[{'label':'Complete proof and conventions','url':'research/n12-t2/index.html'},{'label':'Exact rational certificate','url':'research/n12-t2/certificate.json'},{'label':'Independent tensor verification','url':'research/n12-t2/independent-verification.json'}]},
 {'id':'n10-t2-maximum','title':'Ten qubits: maximal second-order dimension is two','summary':'The paper supplies a two-dimensional exact basis. The tensor-Gram bound excludes a third vector.','formulaLatex':r'4k\le 11\quad\Longrightarrow\quad k\le2', 'bodyHtml':'<p>Combining the exact Eq. E2 basis with the Gram inequality settles this maximum. The paper’s Schmidt bound alone gives three.</p>','references':[ref(12,'E2')]}
])
pure_proofs={p['id']:p for p in pure['proofs']};pure_entries={(e['n'],e['t']):e for e in pure['entries']}
